# {{ project_name }}

{{ description }}

From returns to your firm's tear sheets: returns by horizon, return tables, risk basics, charts,
and a reconciliation against reference figures. It runs as generated, and it is yours to change.

```bash
uv sync
uv run {{ project_name }} report                 # a demo report, in reports/
uv run {{ project_name }} report --tearsheet investor
uv run {{ project_name }} sections               # what you can put on a tear sheet
```

## Use your returns

Set `returns` in `config.toml` to a CSV or Excel file with a date column, then one column of
returns per fund, strategy, or book:

```text
date,Equity L/S,Macro,Index
2024-01-31,0.012,-0.004,0.016
2024-02-29,0.008,0.011,0.042
```

Set `returns_unit` if your figures are percentages, `benchmark` to the column to compare against,
and `basis` to say whether they are net or gross.

## Check it against your administrator

```bash
uv run {{ project_name }} reconcile admin_returns.xlsx --tolerance-bps 1
```

It compares each book month by month, lists every break, and exits 1 if anything fails to agree.

## Make it yours

| Level | What you do | Where |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | Set conventions and data | `config.toml` |
| 2 | Choose each audience's sections | `[tearsheets.*]` in `config.toml` |
| 3 | Add your own sections and file formats | `{{ package_name }}/firm/` |
| 4 | Change anything else; you own the code | Everywhere, with the tests to catch mistakes |

`AGENTS.md` tells a coding assistant the same rules. See `LIMITATIONS.md` for what this does not do.
